.TH GIRLREPO 1 2026-10-11 girlpages "girlpages Manual"
.SH NAME
girlrepo \- build and publish girlpages repositories and their website
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR file ]
.B ingest
.RI [ options ]
.IR path ...
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B ingest \-\-system
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B crawl
.BR apt | rpm | pacman | rfc
.I url
.RI [ suite ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B import
.RI [ options ]
.I source
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B unpack
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B export
.RI [ options ]
.I out
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B girlify
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ name ...]
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B publish
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B archive
.RI [ options ]
.I archive
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.BR gc " | " search " | " serve " | " status
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B girlrepo
collects man pages from packages and systems, girlifies them, and publishes
them as a
.I repository
\(em a tree of JSON files any static web host can serve \(em with a website on
top.
.BR girl (1)
can fetch pages from a repository instead of asking a model, and the website
lets anyone browse and search them, with a plain HTML page for every man page
so search engines can find them.
.PP
.I dir
(the current directory unless
.B \-d
is given) is the website; the repository lives in
.IR dir /repo .
Upload the whole of
.I dir
to publish both.
Every command that changes the repository takes a lock on it, so they can be
run from cron or CI without stepping on each other.
.PP
The usual cycle is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
girlrepo ingest \-\-distro debian \-\-release trixie *.deb
girlrepo girlify
girlrepo publish \-\-url https://example.org \-\-gc
.fi
.RE
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "ingest \fIpath\fR..."
Adds every English man page found in each
.IR path ,
which may be a directory (a manual root such as
.BR /usr/share/man ,
or any tree containing one, like an unpacked package or a whole system
image) or a package file:
.BR .deb ,
.BR .pkg.tar.* ,
or
.B .rpm
(source packages are skipped), the URL of one, or a git repository:
.BI git+ url\fR,
or any URL ending in
.BR .git ,
optionally followed by
.BI # ref
for a branch, tag or commit.
Git repositories are cloned shallowly, and every roff page in them
.RI ( name . section ,
perhaps compressed, starting with
.B .TH
or
.BR .Dt )
is ingested wherever it is, apart from translations; pages generated at build
time from other formats aren't there to be found.
Their package is the repository, at the tag or commit.
Packages are unpacked with
.BR tar (1)
and
.BR cpio (1);
neither dpkg nor rpm is needed.
Translations
.RI ( man/de/man1 ,
and so on) are left out.
.PP
Each page is identified by the SHA-256 of its roff source, decompressed, with
.B .so
requests replaced by what they include, and symlinks followed (inside the
unpacked package, for absolute links).
The same page from different packages or distributions is stored once, noting
everywhere it was found.
A page whose source changed becomes a new version; old versions are kept.
A page's current version, the one the website shows, is its latest by version
number: the upstream version of the packages it was found in (\fB1.35\fR from
both \fB1.35+dfsg\-3.1\fR and \fB1.35\-5\fR; epochs, Debian revisions, rpm
and pacman releases and repacking suffixes don't count), then, for the same
version, the one found in the most places, then the one added last.
New versions are rendered with
.BR man (1)
to make their
.B man
variant.
If
.BR girl (1)'s
cache already has a conversion of the same text, that becomes the
.B girl
variant straight away.
.TP
.B \-\-system
Ingest this system's manpath instead of paths, noting which installed package
(from the dpkg, pacman or rpm database) each page came from, and the
distribution from
.BR os\-release (5).
.TP
.BR \-\-distro =\fIid\fR ", " \-\-release =\fIrelease\fR
Record where the pages came from, such as
.B debian
and
.BR trixie .
.TP
.BR \-\-package =\fIname\fR ", " \-\-package\-version =\fIversion\fR ", " \-\-arch =\fIarch\fR
Record the package, for directories, or for a single package file whose name
doesn't follow its format's convention.
Package files are otherwise identified by their names.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sections =\fIlist\fR
Only these sections.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs =\fIn\fR
Render this many pages at once (default: one per CPU).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Say what would be added.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cache
Don't take conversions from girl's cache.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Only report problems.
.SS "crawl apt \fIurl suite\fR | rpm \fIurl\fR | pacman \fIurl\fR | rfc \fR[\fIurl\fR]"
Ingests every package in a repository that has man pages, or every RFC:
.TP
.B apt \fIurl suite\fR
A Debian-style repository, such as
.B https://deb.debian.org/debian trixie
or
.BR "http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu noble" .
Pages are recorded as from the suite's codename, without any
.B \-updates
or
.B \-security
(so
.B bookworm\-security
is
.BR bookworm ).
Debian's security archive has empty Contents, so for a
.I url
ending
.B \-security
the main archive's say which packages have man pages.
.TP
.B rpm \fIurl\fR
An rpm-md (yum, dnf) repository: the directory holding
.BR repodata/ .
.TP
.B pacman \fIurl\fR
An Arch-style repository: the directory holding the database, such as
.BR https://geo.mirror.pkgbuild.com/core/os/x86_64 .
.TP
.B rfc \fR[\fIurl\fR]
RFCs, from the RFC Editor (or a mirror of
.B https://www.rfc-editor.org/
at
.IR url ),
by its
.BR rfc-index.xml :
each one with text, as page
.I number
in section
.BR rfc ,
its title its description, its text (cleaned of page breaks and running
headers and footers) its original, and
.B ietf
its source.
.B \-\-rfcs
.I numbers
(comma separated) crawls just those.
They're girlified with a prompt of their own, which keeps every BCP 14 key
word (MUST, SHOULD, MAY…) and section number, and on the website live at
.BI rfc/ number /\fR.
.PP
Man pages only exist inside packages, compressed as a whole, so they can't
be fetched on their own.
Instead the repository's file lists (apt's
.BR Contents ,
rpm's
.BR filelists ,
pacman's
.BR .files )
say which packages have English man pages, and only those are downloaded
(checking their SHA-256 against the index), ingested a few at a time with
their package, version and architecture, and deleted.
Only the newest version of each package is taken.
What's been ingested is remembered (in
.BR ~/.cache/girlpages/girlrepo\-crawl/ ),
so crawling again only fetches packages that are new or changed.
Repository signatures aren't checked; use https mirrors.
.TP
.BR \-\-components =\fIlist\fR ", " \-\-arch =\fIarch\fR
For apt: which components (default: all the suite has) and architecture
(default amd64; architecture-independent packages come too).
.TP
.BR \-\-repo =\fIname\fR
For pacman: the database name, if it isn't the directory two above the URL.
.TP
.BR \-\-distro =\fIid\fR ", " \-\-release =\fIrelease\fR
What to record, if not what the repository says.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-match =\fIregexp\fR
Only packages whose names match.
.TP
.BR \-\-limit =\fIn\fR
Stop after this many packages.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs =\fIn\fR
Download this many packages at once (default 4).
.TP
.BR \-\-group =\fIn\fR
Ingest this many packages together (default 16); a page can only include
pages from packages in its group.
.TP
.B \-\-again
Ingest packages even if an earlier crawl did.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-versions
Take every version the repository has, not just the newest.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Download every package if the repository has no file lists.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
List the packages that would be fetched, and how much that is.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-no\-cache ", " \-q
As for
.BR ingest .
.SS "import \fIsource\fR"
Merges another published girlpages repository into this one.
.I source
is a URL or a directory: the repository itself, or a site with it in
.BR repo/ .
Pages this repository doesn't have are added whole; versions it doesn't have
are added to its pages, keeping when and where they were found; versions both
have gain each other's packages and any variants this one lacks.
Pages whose listings are already identical are skipped, and every file
fetched is checked against the hash its listing gives, so a damaged or
tampered file is refused (and reported) rather than imported.
Run
.B publish
afterwards.
.PP
If the other repository has packs (see
.BR publish ),
it's synced by them: the packs this repository hasn't had from it are
fetched (a few big files, rather than a request for every one), checked,
and unpacked into a copy of it kept in the cache, and the pages they
changed merged from there.
The next import fetches just the packs added since.
.TP
.B \-\-prefer\-remote
When both have a variant of the same version and they differ (two girl
conversions, usually), take theirs; otherwise this repository's is kept.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sections =\fIlist\fR ", " \-m ", " \-\-match =\fIregexp\fR
Only these sections, or pages whose names match.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs =\fIn\fR
Fetch this many pages at once (default 8).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Say what would be imported.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-packs
Fetch its files one by one, even if it has packs.
.TP
.B \-\-remerge
Merge every page in its packs again, not just those that changed since the
last merge.
Pages whose listings are the same here are still checked: if any file a
listing names is missing (a merge stopped part of the way, or a file system
that lost what it was writing), it's fetched again.
Any merge does that for the pages it merges; this one merges them all.
.B publish
leaves versions whose files are missing out of the search index, and says
how many there are; this is what puts them back.
.SS "unpack [\fIpath\fR]"
Merges packs that are already here into this repository, as
.B import
does with packs it fetches: those in
.IR path ,
a pack-only repository (as
.B export
writes) or a directory of pack files, or with no
.IR path ,
those dropped into
.IR dir /repo/packs/
(but not this repository's own).
Packs in
.IR dir /repo/packs/
are moved out of the way as they're unpacked; those in
.I path
are left as they are.
It takes
.BR import 's
options, but for
.BR \-\-no\-packs .
With
.BR \-\-remerge ,
give the packs'
.IR path ,
as those moved out of
.IR dir /repo/packs/
aren't there any more.
.SS "export \fIout\fR"
Writes the repository as packs into
.IR out ,
a pack-only repository: its packs, and a
.B manifest.json
listing them, for
.B import
or
.B unpack
elsewhere (or serving).
If
.I out
has packs already, the changes since its last are added, as
.B publish
adds to the repository's own.
.TP
.BR \-\-pack\-size =\fIMiB\fR
How big full packs get (default 256).
.SS "girlify [\fIname\fR...]"
Converts the current (latest) version of every page (or just the named ones: a name for any section, or
.IB name ( section )
for one) that has
no
.B girl
variant yet, using the backend and model from girl's configuration (see
.BR girl (1)).
Aliases sharing a source are converted once.
Interrupting it is safe: each page is saved as it's done, and the next run
carries on.
.TP
.BR \-\-variant =\fIname\fR
Make this variant instead of
.BR girl :
the pages are rewritten in its voice, and each gets it alongside its
others.
.B \-\-list\-variants
lists them:
.B boy
and
.B femboy
(who keep every detail, as
.B girl
does),
.B lolcat
and
.B pirate
(every detail, in a dialect),
.B eli5
and
.B woman
(every detail, plus explanations or stories), and
.B witch
and
.B bofh
(every literal and warning, but perhaps only the gist of the rest).
Only
.B girl
takes conversions from girl's cache, and each variant has its own prompt
versions, for
.BR \-\-reprompt .
.TP
.BR \-\-provider =\fIname\fR
Use the provider configured as
.BI [providers. name ]
in girl's configuration (see
.BR girl (1)).
.TP
.BR \-\-backend =\fIname\fR ", " \-\-model =\fImodel\fR
Override the configured backend and model.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs =\fIn\fR
Send this many requests at once (default from the configuration): to as
many pages, or, when fewer pages are left, to the parts of long ones.
Long pages report their progress every half minute, and each part is kept
as it's converted, so a page that fails part way only needs its missing
parts the next time.
.TP
.BR \-\-limit =\fIn\fR
Stop after this many pages.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sections =\fIlist\fR
Only these sections.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-versions
Convert old versions too.
.TP
.BR \-\-batch =\fIapi\fR ", " \-\-no\-batch
Send pages in batches with
.BR openrouter ", " anthropic " or " sail ,
or not at all, overriding the
.B batch
setting.
.B \-\-batch\-requests
.I n
puts at most
.I n
requests in each batch (overriding
.BR batch_max_requests ),
so results come back in smaller lots.
Batches work as for
.BR "girl \-\-bulk" ;
see
.BR girl (1).
What was sent is saved in
.BR ~/.cache/girlpages/girlrepo\-batches/ ,
and the next
.B girlify
collects whatever has finished.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
Send batches off and return without waiting.
.TP
.B \-\-reprompt
Convert again versions whose variant was made with an older prompt.
Listings record each variant's prompt version, and
.B status
counts them.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Convert again, even pages that already have a
.B girl
variant.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
List what would be girlified, with a rough count of the requests and tokens
it would take: about four bytes a token, and output
.B \-\-output\-ratio
(default 1.2, measured from real conversions) times as long as the original.
Thinking tokens, for models that think, aren't counted.
.TP
.BR \-\-price =\fIin\fR/\fIout\fR
With
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
estimate the cost at these US dollars per million input and output tokens,
both live and in batches at half price.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-cache ", " \-q ", " \-\-quiet
As for
.BR ingest .
.SS publish
Writes the section indexes, the search index and
.BR manifest.json ,
then the website.
Until it runs, nothing ingested or girlified since the last publish is
visible.
Website files are only rewritten when they change, so syncing the result
somewhere is cheap.
.PP
The website's static pages are what search engines should see: the home
page, the section listings (in pages of 200,
.IB section /page/ n /\fR,
which the browser scrolls through as one long list, keeping only what's near
the screen), and each page's current version, girlified if it
has been (otherwise the original), with canonical links, OpenGraph and Twitter
card tags, JSON-LD (TechArticle and BreadcrumbList for pages, WebSite with a
SearchAction for the home page, CollectionPage for listings) and a sitemap.
.PP
Each page's static page is at
.IB girl / section / name /
.RB ( girl/1/ls/ ,
.B girl/rfc/9110/
for a series' document), girlified (or the original, until it is).
In any other variant it's at
.IB variant / section / name /
.RB ( man/1/ls/
for the original,
.BR pirate/1/ls/ ):
nothing's pre-rendered there, and servers send those (and listings,
searches and the home page in a variant:
.BR pirate/section/1/ ,
.BR pirate/ )
the app's shell,
.BR static/shell.html ,
which the app draws the page in; listings are at
.IB section / key /
.RB ( section/1/ ,
.BR section/rfc/ ).
.B serve
and
.BR girlserve (1)
know a site's variants from its manifest, and send a series' old addresses
.RB ( rfc/9110/ ,
.BR rfc/ )
where they are now;
.B dist/hosting/
has the same rules for nginx and static-web-server.
A reader can make a variant their default: pages linked to (and girly pages
opened) are in it from then on, where they have it.
.PP
Everything else happens in the browser, read straight from the repository by
an app in the home page's hash:
older versions
.RB ( #/1/ls/9.4 ,
by upstream version or the start of a source hash),
and browsing by where pages come from, a distribution
.RB ( #/debian ),
a release of one
.RB ( #/debian/trixie/section/1 ,
.BR #/debian/trixie/1/ls )
or
.B upstream
for git repositories, each showing the version of every page that source
has.
.B /search/
searches every page from every source, all of their versions' text, and can
keep to one source; by words, or by parts of words.
Without
.B \-\-url
there are no absolute URLs, so no canonical links, OpenGraph URLs or sitemap.
.TP
.BR \-\-url =\fIurl\fR
The site's absolute URL, for canonical links and
.BR sitemap.xml .
.TP
.BR \-\-base =\fIpath\fR
The URL path the site is served from, if not
.BR / ,
such as
.B /girlpages/
for a GitHub Pages project site.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-site
Only update the repository.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs =\fIn\fR
Write this many files at once (default one per CPU).
On a network file system (CephFS, NFS), where each file is mostly a wait
for the server, many more than that (32, say) can be much quicker.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-packs
Don't add to the repository's packs.
Otherwise, each publish adds the changes since the last to
.IR dir /repo/packs/:
the repository's files (but not its indexes, which are worked out from
them) as a log of packs, zstd-compressed tar files of about 256 MiB, for
mirroring it without a request for every file.
The changes since the last full pack are kept in small tail packs, which
are replaced by full packs once they'd make one; full packs never change.
The first publish packs everything, which takes a while.
.TP
.BR \-\-packs =\fIpath\fR
List the packs in
.I path
(a directory of pack files, as
.B unpack
takes) as the repository's, in place of its own, adding nothing to them.
For a repository made by unpacking just those packs, as a publishing job
does from scratch each time: it's a copy of the one they came from, so its
log is theirs, and publishing that keeps the site's packs the same from one
publish to the next.
.TP
.BR \-\-from\-packs =\fIpath\fR
Publish the repository the packs in
.I path
make, and its website, straight into
.B \-\-archive
(which it needs), with no repository in
.IR dir ,
or anything else written anywhere: their files are replayed into the
archive's new version as they're read, the indexes and website made from
there, and the packs listed as the repository's (as with
.BR \-\-packs ).
It's what
.B unpack
then
.B publish \-\-packs \-\-archive
make, without the millions of files between, so a job publishing packs
needs no scratch space.
.TP
.BR \-\-archive =\fIdir\fR
Add the website (and repository) to the archive in
.I dir
afterwards: see
.BR archive .
The archive compresses what it adds, so
.B \-\-precompress
defaults to
.B none
with it.
It takes
.BR archive 's
options too.
.TP
.B \-\-gc
Collect garbage afterwards.
.TP
.BR \-\-precompress =\fIlist\fR
Write compressed copies of the site's and repository's files, in these
formats (default
.BR zstd,br,gzip ;
.B none
for none): see
.BR HOSTING .
Only new and changed files are compressed, and copies of removed files go.
.TP
.BR \-\-compress\-jobs =\fIn\fR
Compress this many files at once (default: as many as --jobs).
.PP
The website is a progressive web app: it can be installed, and a service
worker keeps the app itself (scripts, styles, and the browse, search and
offline pages) for offline use.
Repository files fetched by the browser are kept in its Cache API:
content-addressed ones are read from there without asking the network, and
the files that change are kept as a fallback for when there's no network.
Offline, the service worker sends static pages to the browse app, which
renders them from those files: every page read before, every page of a
section saved with its listing's
.B save for offline
button, and any search, once the search page's
.B search offline
button has fetched the search index (and its
.B typo matching offline
button the index that catches typos), with a switch to show only the pages
that can be read offline (on by default when offline).
Every half hour while a page is open, the browser looks at
.BR manifest.json ;
when it has changed, the search index files and section indexes the new one
doesn't use are deleted in idle time.
.SS gc
Deletes files nothing refers to any more: superseded listings, section indexes
and index shards, and the contents of versions no listing mentions.
Content-addressed files may be cached by browsers and proxies forever, so it is
kinder to collect garbage some time after publishing rather than straight
away.
.B \-n
says what would go.
.SS "search \fIquery\fR..."
Searches the published index the way the website does.
.B \-\-repo
searches a repository elsewhere;
.B \-l
sets how many results, and
.B \-\-ngrams
finds the words query words are anywhere inside of
.RB ( dir
finds
.BR mkdir ),
as the website's "parts of words" does, rather than only the words they
start.
.SS "archive \fIarchive\fR"
Adds the website in
.I dir
(and the repository in it) to the archive in
.I archive
(making it, if it's not there), as its new version: a few big files to
serve the site from, made for a network file system, where a site's
millions of small files are slow.
It holds
.IR segments ,
files of up to a gigabyte of the site's files' bytes, each stored as it's
sent (as it is, and compressed with zstd, brotli and gzip, where that's
smaller); an
.I index
for each version, of where each file's bytes are; and
.BR current ,
naming the version being served, which
.B serve \-\-archive
and
.BR girlserve (1)
watch.
Only files whose content isn't in the archive already are compressed and
added: the rest of the new version points at where they are.
Versions replaced are kept (at least
.B \-\-keep
of them, and any newer than
.BR \-\-keep\-for ),
for servers still on one, and then they, and the segments only they used, go.
The repository's contents and what's generated from them (its indexes and
the website, which change each publish) are kept in segments of their own,
and when less than half of either's bytes are in use, it's compacted: what
is, is copied into new segments, and the other's left alone.
One
.B archive
(or
.BR "publish \-\-archive" )
runs at a time.
The format is in
.B doc/archive-format.md
in the source.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs =\fIn\fR
Compress this many files at once (default one per CPU).
.TP
.B \-\-compact
Compact it now, whatever its state.
.TP
.BR \-\-keep =\fIn\fR ", " \-\-keep\-for =\fIduration\fR
Keep at least
.I n
versions (default 5), and any replaced less than
.I duration
ago (default 1h).
.SS serve
Serves
.I dir
(or, with
.BR \-\-archive ,
an archive) at
.B http://localhost:1990/
(or
.BR \-\-listen ).
It serves the way a production server should (each file in the smallest form
the client accepts, with its cache lifetime, ETags and the site's 404 page),
and takes the options of
.BR girlserve (1),
which is the same server on its own:
.BR \-\-archive ,
.BR \-\-admin\-listen ,
.BR \-\-reload\-interval ,
.BI \-\-access\-log= file
(a file, or
.B \-
for standard output),
.B \-\-drain
and
.BR \-\-drain\-delay
(default 0 here).
.SS status
Counts pages, versions and conversions.
.SH "REPOSITORY FORMAT"
This is a summary: the full specification, for writing tools that read or
write repositories, is
.I doc/repo-format.md
in the girlpages source.
.PP
Everything is JSON.
Files named
.IB name \- sha .json
or
.IB sha .json
are named after the SHA-256 of their own contents and never change, so they
can be cached forever.
.B manifest.json
and the pointer files change in place and should be cached briefly.
.TP
.B manifest.json
The root: sections, page counts and the index manifests.
.TP
.BI contents/ ab / source /variants.json
Every generation of every variant of one version of a page: each variant
made again (with a new prompt, say) is a new generation, and the latest is
the highest.
.TP
.BI contents/ ab / source / variant \- sha .json
One generation of a variant, named after its own hash.
.TP
.BI page/name\- section / name .json
Points at the page's listing.
.TP
.BI page/page\- ab / sha .json
Every version of a page, where each was found, and its variants' hashes.
.TP
.BI section/ section .json
Points at the section's index.
.TP
.BI section/ section \- sha .json
Every page in a section, with its description and listing hash.
.TP
.BI index/words/manifest\- sha .json
The range of words in each word shard.
.TP
.BI index/words/shard\- ab / sha .json
Words, each with the pages it appears in as [doc id, score].
.TP
.BI index/grams/manifest\- sha .json
The range of trigrams in each gram shard.
.TP
.BI index/grams/shard\- ab / sha .json
Trigrams, each with the words containing it, for finding misspelt words.
.TP
.BI index/docs/manifest\- sha .json
The shards of the page table that doc ids index.
.TP
.BI index/docs/shard\- ab / sha .json
Part of the page table: [name, section, description, sources].
.TP
.BI index/variants/ variant /manifest\- sha .json
The range of keys in each of a variant's index shards.
.TP
.BI index/variants/ variant /shard\- ab / sha .json
The first 16 hex digits of the source hash of every version with that
variant, sorted, so a client like
.B girl \-\-bulk
or
.B girl \-\-import
can tell which of its pages a repository has without asking for each.
.B manifest.json
names each variant's index under
.BR index.variants ,
and lists the variants under
.BR variants .
.PP
.I source
is the page's source hash (as above) and
.I ab
its first two characters.
Variants are
.B girl
(girlpages markup, split into sections at top-level headings) and
.B man
(man's plain text, split at its headings, with the header and footer lines
apart), and there may be others: any short lower-case name
.RB ( [a\-z][a\-z0\-9\-]* ),
for pages rewritten with other prompts, each saying its
.B syntax
so clients know how to show it; joining a variant's sections gives back exactly the original.
Names and sections are escaped in file names: anything but letters, digits
and
.B _+\-.,:@
(or a leading dot) becomes
.BI % XX\fR,
which must be percent-encoded again in URLs.
.PP
A client with a page installed needs one request:
.IB repo /contents/ ab / source /girl.json\fR.
The website's search reads the manifests, then only the shards a query needs.
.SH HOSTING
A site is plain files: any static web server will do.
.B publish
also writes compressed copies of every file worth compressing
.RB ( .zst ,
.BR .br " and"
.BR .gz ;
see
.BR \-\-precompress ),
compressed as hard as each format goes, once, so the server only has to send
the right one: static-web-server with
.BR \-\-compression\-static ,
or nginx with
.BR gzip_static ,
.B brotli_static
and
.BR zstd_static .
JSON shrinks to about a quarter.
.BR girl (1)
asks for zstd, then brotli, then gzip, and browsers ask for what they can
take.
Ready-made configurations are in the source,
.B dist/hosting/static\-web\-server.toml
and
.BR dist/hosting/nginx.conf .
.PP
They send these
.BR Cache\-Control s,
as
.B girlrepo serve
does:
.TP
.B public, max-age=31536000, immutable
Files named after their own hash: listings
.RB ( repo/page/page\-*/ ),
section indexes
.RB ( repo/section/*\- sha .json ),
variants
.RB ( repo/contents/*/*/ variant \- sha .json ),
and everything under
.BR repo/index/ .
.TP
.B public, max-age=300
.BR repo/manifest.json ,
and the pointers into the rest
.RB ( repo/page/name\-*/ ,
.BR repo/section/ section .json ,
.BR repo/contents/*/*/variants.json ),
which change on every publish.
.TP
.B no-cache
.BR sw.js ,
so browsers pick up a new site.
.TP
.B public, max-age=3600
Everything else.
.PP
A CDN such as CloudFront can go in front, keeping those lifetimes (set its
cache policy to use the origin's headers) and passing
.B Accept\-Encoding
through to the origin so its copies are used; CloudFront normalises that
header to the encodings it knows, so check zstd is among them, or clients
behind it will get brotli.
Files a publish removes are best left until clients' caches have moved on:
publish, sync, and collect garbage
.RB ( gc )
a few minutes later, rather than all at once.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR dir /repo/
The repository.
.TP
.IR dir /repo/.lock
Held while the repository is being changed.
.TP
.IR dir /repo/packs/
The repository's packs, and
.BR index.json ,
which lists them.
.TP
.B ~/.cache/girlpages/girlrepo\-sync/
Copies of the repositories
.B import
and
.B unpack
have synced packs from: their packs, and what unpacking them gives.
.TP
.B ~/.cache/girlpages/girlrepo\-parts/
Parts of long pages
.B girlify
has converted, until their page is done.
.TP
.IR dir /man/ section / name /
A page's current version on the website.
.TP
.IR dir /browse/
Sends links from earlier versions of the browser app to where it is now.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on success, 1 for a usage error, 2 if something failed, 130 if interrupted.
.PP
Ctrl+C stops any command: the first asks it to stop, which it does as soon
as it's safe (a publish leaves the repository as it was published last),
and a second stops it right away.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B girlrepo ingest \-\-system && girlrepo girlify \-\-limit 100 && girlrepo publish
Start a repository from this machine, girlifying the first hundred pages.
.TP
.B girlrepo ingest \-\-distro arch \-\-release rolling /var/cache/pacman/pkg/*.pkg.tar.zst
Add every page from Arch's package cache.
.TP
.B girlrepo publish \-\-base /girlpages/ \-\-url https://naomiamethyst.github.io/girlpages
Build for a GitHub Pages project site.
.TP
.B girlrepo crawl apt https://deb.debian.org/debian trixie \-\-components main \-n
See how much of Debian has man pages, and how big those packages are.
.TP
.B girlrepo ingest git+https://github.com/tmux/tmux#3.5a
Add tmux's page as of its 3.5a tag.
.TP
.B girlrepo girlify \-n \-q \-s 1 \-\-price 0.10/0.50
Estimate what girlifying section 1 would cost.
.TP
.B girlrepo export /srv/girlpages\-packs
.PD 0
.TP
.B girlrepo \-d mirror import https://example.org/girlpages\-packs
.PD
Write the repository as packs, then on another machine, merge them into a
repository of its own (and again later, fetching just what's new).
.TP
.B girl \-\-repo ./repo ls
Read
.BR ls (1)
from a repository on disk.
